From ba9ed76a1a145216a190e8dd9a7f84a96e7f6b2b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zapleoceo <zaporozec_d@itstep.org>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 19:09:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(vision): deny swap to vision-local, the missing half of
 the OOM fix
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The 2026-09-02 note in this file records the mechanism correctly — "the
cgroup cap never fired, the HOST ran out" — but not why the cap never
fired. Docker without memswap_limit grants twice the memory limit in swap,
so vision-local stayed under its 5600m of RAM while draining all 4GB of
host swap. Only once swap was gone did the kernel run out globally and
choose a victim across the whole box: 27 oom-kills over 02-03.09, with
postgres as eligible as llama-server. It also spammed the owner with 14
RAM alerts in five hours, because 88-91% is simply what this box looks
like while the model is resident.

memswap_limit == mem_limit denies swap. The cgroup then reclaims its own
page cache under pressure instead of pushing the host into global OOM, and
an over-limit request is killed inside its own cgroup: one image fails,
the container restarts, nothing else is touched.

-c and mem_limit are deliberately untouched. 4096 would truncate the
largest document actually served (MAX=4451 tokens) and 3800m was measured
to thrash — both are recorded above and both were briefly, wrongly changed
before this commit.

## Evidence

Applied live on 2026-09-03 before this commit, then lost to a deploy
(edited in the working tree without committing — the reason for this
commit). Measured over the 4 hours it was in effect:

  memory.swap.max in the container cgroup: 0
  host available RAM:  754 MB -> 3562 MB
  host swap:           4095/4095 -> 2539/4095 and draining
  oom kills, host:     27 in 48h -> 0
  oom kills, cgroup:   0
  vision-local:        healthy, serving

After the deploy reverted it, swap was back to 3974/4095 within hours and
llama-server was holding 1505 MB of it.

Known remaining exposure, not addressed here: ten containers still run
with no mem_limit at all (aibroker api/monitor/pgbouncer/postgres/redis,
all five stepan2 services), and the host is still slightly over-committed
at 5600m + ~2.2GB on 7.56GB.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 docker-compose.yml | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/docker-compose.yml b/docker-compose.yml
index c6e9d57..a4131df 100644
--- a/docker-compose.yml
+++ b/docker-compose.yml
@@ -156,6 +156,22 @@ services:
       - ${VISION_MODEL_DIR:-/var/lib/aibroker-vision/model}:/models:ro
     cpus: 2.5
     mem_limit: 5600m
+    # memswap_limit == mem_limit means NO swap for this container. Without it
+    # docker grants 2x the limit (11.2GB) in swap, and that is the missing half
+    # of the 2026-09-02 note above: the cgroup cap 'never fired' because the
+    # container stayed under 5600m of RAM while draining all 4GB of HOST swap.
+    # Only then did the kernel run out globally and pick a victim across the
+    # whole box (global_oom, 27 kills 02-03.09; postgres was as eligible as
+    # llama-server). With swap denied the cgroup reclaims its own page cache
+    # first, and an over-limit request is killed inside its own cgroup: one
+    # image fails, the container restarts, nothing else is touched.
+    #
+    # -c and mem_limit are deliberately left exactly as measured above. The
+    # host stays slightly over-committed at 5600m + ~2.2GB of other services
+    # on 7.56GB; the remaining levers are the ten containers that still have
+    # no mem_limit at all (aibroker api/monitor/pgbouncer/postgres/redis and
+    # all five stepan2 services), not this one.
+    memswap_limit: 5600m
     expose:
       - "8080"
     # /health is exempt from llama-server's idle timer, so polling it can
